                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the counts for multiples of 3 and 5, then subtract the 6 common multiples of 15.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $A$ be the event of divisibility by 3 and $B$ the event of divisibility by 5. There are 33 multiples of 3, 20 multiples of 5 and 6 common multiples, which are multiples of 15.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(A\\cup B)&=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B)\\\\P(A\\cup B)&=\\frac{33}{100}+\\frac{20}{100}-\\frac{6}{100}\\\\P(A\\cup B)&=\\frac{47}{100}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
